 void CertVerifierServiceImpl::OnCertVerifierChanged() {
+  // Stedding (BACKLOG S-251): the client answers a change by dropping what
+  // the old verifier let through: its cached results, and the sockets of its
+  // network context -- every idle one, plain http too, and every one still
+  // being opened ahead of a page (a preconnect). Before this service has
+  // verified a certificate nothing was let through, and the call only threw
+  // those sockets away. It did at every launch: a few hundred milliseconds
+  // in, the profile's first update (UpdateAdditionalCertificates, which a
+  // `waiting_for_update_` service waits for) and the factory's network time
+  // (UpdateNetworkTime) arrived after the tabs given on the command line had
+  // each connected ahead and before their requests left. Each tab then opened
+  // a new connection at the same moment; a server with a short accept queue
+  // (Python's http.server: five) had some of them reset, a fresh connection
+  // reset before its response is not retried, and those tabs, in the
+  // background, stayed on ERR_CONNECTION_RESET.
+  if (!verified_any_) {
+    return;
+  }
   client_->OnCertVerifierChanged();
 }
 
